example.source:
teams = ["red", "blue"]
items = ["a", "b"]
found = "none"
for t in teams:
    for v in items:
        if t == "red":
            if v == "a":
                found = "red-a"
                break
    print(t)
print(found)

units.explanation:
```python
teams = ["red", "blue"]
items = ["a", "b"]
for t in teams:
    for v in items:
        if t == "red" and v == "a":
            break
    print(t)
```
The outer list is `teams`; `t` selects `"red"` then `"blue"`. The inner list is `items`; `v` selects `"a"` then `"b"`. When `t` is `"red"` and `v` is `"a"`, `break` exits only the `v` loop. Control resumes after that inner loop, runs `print(t)`, then the outer `t` loop advances to `"blue"` [hub-cs50-loops-0015][hub-cs50-loops-0039].

